<body>
<div id="bar"></div>
<nav id="nav"><b>Quadratics</b></nav>

<header>
<h1>QUADRATIC FUNCTIONS</h1>
<div class="sub">An easy step-by-step review</div>
<p><span class="pill">Changing forms</span><span class="pill">Making an x-y table</span><span class="pill">Graphing a parabola</span></p>
<p style="font-size:14px;margin-top:22px">Based on the provided Day 5 and Day 6 lesson pages</p>
</header>

<main>

<section id="learn" data-nav="Goals">
<h2><small>Overview</small>What will we learn?</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>1. Change the form</h3><p>Rewrite a quadratic equation in another form.</p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>2. Understand the parts</h3><p>Identify a, h, k, and the perfect-square pattern.</p></div>
<div class="card y"><h3>3. Draw the graph</h3><p>Make a table, plot points, and connect them smoothly.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="big">Big idea: A quadratic function makes a curved graph called a PARABOLA.</div>
</section>

<section id="forms" data-nav="Two forms">
<h2><small>Part 1</small>A quadratic function has two common forms</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Vertex form <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--muted)">(also called standard form in your module)</span></h3><div class="eq">y = a(x − h)² + k</div><p>It helps us identify the vertex: (h, k).</p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>General form</h3><div class="eq">y = ax² + bx + c</div><p>It shows the x² term, x term, and constant.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note">Remember: a, b, c, h, and k are numbers. The value of a cannot be zero.</div>

<h2 style="margin-top:36px;font-size:26px">What does vertex form tell us?</h2>
<div class="eq" style="text-align:center">y = a(x − h)² + k</div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>The vertex is (h, k)</h3><p>h tells the x-coordinate.</p><p>k tells the y-coordinate.</p><p>The vertex is the turning point of the parabola.</p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>If a is positive (+)</h3><p>The parabola opens UP like a smile.</p></div>
<div class="card r"><h3>If a is negative (−)</h3><p>The parabola opens DOWN like an upside-down smile.</p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="general-to-vertex" data-nav="General → Vertex">
<h2><small>Part 2</small>General form → Vertex form</h2>
<p class="lead">Example: <span class="mono">f(x) = x² − 10x + 25</span></p>
<div class="steps">
<div class="step"><h3>Look for a perfect square</h3><p class="mono">x² − 10x + 25</p><p>Find two equal factors of x² and 25: <span class="mono">(x − 5)(x − 5)</span></p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Write it as a square</h3><p class="mono">(x − 5)(x − 5) = (x − 5)²</p></div>
</div>
<div class="final">Answer: f(x) = (x − 5)²</div>
<p class="lead">Why? (x − 5)² means (x − 5) multiplied by itself.</p>

<h3 style="margin-top:30px">How do we recognize a perfect square?</h3>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Pattern 1: plus</h3><div class="eq">x² + 2ax + a² = (x + a)²</div><p>Example: x² + 6x + 9 = (x + 3)²</p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>Pattern 2: minus</h3><div class="eq">x² − 2ax + a² = (x − a)²</div><p>Example: x² − 10x + 25 = (x − 5)²</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Quick check:</b> the first and last terms must be squares, and the middle term must match the pattern.</div>

<h3 style="margin-top:30px">Example with a number in front</h3>
<p class="lead mono">f(x) = 2x² + 24x + 72</p>
<div class="steps">
<div class="step"><h3>Take out the common factor 2.</h3><p class="mono">2(x² + 12x + 36)</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Factor the trinomial inside the parentheses.</h3><p class="mono">x² + 12x + 36 = (x + 6)(x + 6)</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Write the repeated factors as a square.</h3></div>
</div>
<div class="final">Final answer: f(x) = 2(x + 6)²</div>
</section>

<section id="vertex-to-general" data-nav="Vertex → General">
<h2><small>Part 3</small>Vertex form → General form</h2>
<p class="lead">Example: <span class="mono">y = 3(x − 2)² + 4</span></p>
<div class="steps">
<div class="step"><h3>Expand the square</h3><p class="mono">(x − 2)² = (x − 2)(x − 2) = x² − 4x + 4</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Multiply everything by 3</h3><p class="mono">y = 3(x² − 4x + 4) + 4<br>y = 3x² − 12x + 12 + 4</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Combine the constants</h3><p class="mono">12 + 4 = 16</p></div>
</div>
<div class="final">Final answer: y = 3x² − 12x + 16</div>

<h3 style="margin-top:30px">Another example: (x + 3)² − 5</h3>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><p class="mono">f(x) = (x + 3)² − 5</p><p>Expand the square:</p><p class="mono">(x + 3)² = x² + 6x + 9</p></div>
<div class="card g"><p>Then subtract 5:</p><p class="mono">f(x) = x² + 6x + 9 − 5<br>f(x) = x² + 6x + 4</p></div>
</div>
<div class="final">Final answer: f(x) = x² + 6x + 4</div>
</section>

<section id="exercises" data-nav="Exercises">
<h2><small>Practice together</small>Let’s solve Exercise 5</h2>
<div class="tabs"><button class="on" data-t="a">Exercise 5a</button><button data-t="b">Exercise 5b</button></div>
<div id="t-a">
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>A. f(x) = x² − 14x + 49</h3><p>49 = 7² and −14x = −2(7)x</p><p class="mono">x² − 14x + 49 = (x − 7)²</p><p><b>Answer: f(x) = (x − 7)²</b></p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>B. f(x) = x² + 12x + 36</h3><p>36 = 6² and 12x = 2(6)x</p><p class="mono">x² + 12x + 36 = (x + 6)²</p><p><b>Answer: f(x) = (x + 6)²</b></p></div>
</div>
<div class="note">Both are perfect-square trinomials: the first and last terms are square numbers.</div>
</div>
<div id="t-b" hidden>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>A. f(x) = 2(x + 4)²</h3><p>Expand: <span class="mono">(x + 4)² = x² + 8x + 16</span></p><p>Multiply each term by 2:</p><p><b>f(x) = 2x² + 16x + 32</b></p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>B. f(x) = (x + 2)² − 5</h3><p>Expand: <span class="mono">(x + 2)² = x² + 4x + 4</span></p><p>Subtract 5: <span class="mono">x² + 4x + 4 − 5</span></p><p><b>Answer: f(x) = x² + 4x − 1</b></p></div>
</div>
<div class="note">Tip: Expand the parentheses first. Then multiply or combine the numbers outside.</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="graphing" data-nav="Graphing">
<h2><small>Part 4</small>How do we graph a quadratic function?</h2>
<p class="lead">Example from your lesson: <span class="mono">f(x) = x² − 6x + 7</span></p>
<div class="steps">
<div class="step"><h3>Choose several x-values.</h3><p>Use values around the middle of the graph.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Find y for each x.</h3><p>Substitute each x-value into the equation.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Plot and connect.</h3><p>Plot each (x, y) point and connect them with a smooth curve.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note">A quadratic graph is a parabola. It is curved, not a set of straight line segments.</div>

<h3 style="margin-top:30px">Make the x-y table <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--muted)">(tap a column to see its point)</span></h3>
<p class="mono">y = x² − 6x + 7</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table id="tbl"></table></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><p class="mono">When x = 2: y = 2² − 6(2) + 7<br>= 4 − 12 + 7 = −1</p></div>
<div class="card g"><p class="mono">When x = 3: y = 3² − 6(3) + 7<br>= 9 − 18 + 7 = −2</p></div>
</div>
<p class="lead">Each column gives one point: (0, 7), (1, 2), (2, −1), and so on.</p>

<h3>Plot the points and connect them</h3>
<div class="graphbox"><svg id="graph" viewBox="0 0 640 420" role="img" aria-label="Graph of y = x² − 6x + 7, a parabola with vertex (3, −2)"></svg></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Plot the ordered pairs</h3><p>For example, (3, −2) means x = 3 and y = −2.</p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>Connect smoothly</h3><p>Draw one continuous curved line through the points.</p></div>
<div class="card r"><h3>Do not join with corners</h3><p>A parabola has a smooth U-shape.</p></div>
</div>

<h3 style="margin-top:30px">Understand the graph</h3>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Vertex: (3, −2)</h3><p>The lowest point of this U-shaped graph.</p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>Axis of symmetry: x = 3</h3><p>An imaginary vertical line that splits the graph into two matching halves.</p></div>
<div class="card y"><h3>Opens upward</h3><p>The curve goes down to the vertex, then rises.</p></div>
<div class="card r"><h3>Minimum value: −2</h3><p>The smallest y-value is −2.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Domain:</b> all real x-values. &nbsp; <b>Range:</b> y ≥ −2.</div>

<h3 style="margin-top:30px">A useful shortcut: find the vertex first</h3>
<div class="eq">y = x² − 6x + 7 = (x − 3)² − 2</div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Read the vertex</h3><p>(x − 3)² − 2 gives vertex (3, −2).</p></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>Check the graph</h3><p>The lowest point on the graph is exactly (3, −2).</p></div>
</div>
<div class="big">Vertex form makes the turning point easy to see.</div>
</section>

<section id="mistakes" data-nav="Mistakes">
<h2><small>Watch out</small>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card r"><h3>Mistake 1: Forgetting to square both terms</h3><p>(x + 3)² is x² + 6x + 9, not x² + 9.</p></div>
<div class="card r"><h3>Mistake 2: Not multiplying every term</h3><p>3(x² − 4x + 4) = 3x² − 12x + 12.</p></div>
<div class="card r"><h3>Mistake 3: Mixing up coordinates</h3><p>(3, −2): x comes first, y comes second.</p></div>
<div class="card r"><h3>Mistake 4: Drawing straight segments</h3><p>Use a smooth curve to make the parabola.</p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="practice" data-nav="Practice">
<h2><small>Your turn</small>Quick practice</h2>
<p class="lead">Try these on your own. Pause before checking the answers. Remember: show your steps, not only the final answer.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>A. Change to vertex form</h3><p class="mono">x² + 8x + 16 = ?</p><button class="reveal">Show answer</button><div class="ans mono">(x + 4)²</div></div>
<div class="card g"><h3>B. Change to general form</h3><p class="mono">2(x − 3)² + 1 = ?</p><button class="reveal">Show answer</button><div class="ans mono">2x² − 12x + 19</div></div>
<div class="card y"><h3>C. Identify the vertex</h3><p class="mono">y = (x + 4)² − 2</p><button class="reveal">Show answer</button><div class="ans mono">Vertex = (−4, −2)</div></div>
<div class="card r"><h3>D. Find the y-value</h3><p>If y = x² − 6x + 7 and x = 4, what is y?</p><button class="reveal">Show answer</button><div class="ans mono">y = 4² − 6(4) + 7 = −1</div></div>
</div>
</section>
</main>

<footer id="remember">
<h2>LET’S REMEMBER!</h2>
<ul>
<li>• Vertex form: <span class="mono">y = a(x − h)² + k</span></li>
<li>• General form: <span class="mono">y = ax² + bx + c</span></li>
<li>• To expand: multiply the binomial by itself.</li>
<li>• To graph: make a table → plot points → draw a smooth curve.</li>
<li>• A quadratic graph is called a PARABOLA.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can do it—one step at a time!</p>
</footer>

<script>
// Nav built from sections
const nav=document.getElementById('nav'),secs=[...document.querySelectorAll('section[data-nav]')];
secs.forEach(s=>{const a=document.createElement('a');a.href='#'+s.id;a.textContent=s.dataset.nav;nav.appendChild(a)});
const links=[...nav.querySelectorAll('a')];
function onScroll(){
  const h=document.documentElement,max=h.scrollHeight-h.clientHeight;
  document.getElementById('bar').style.width=(max>0?h.scrollTop/max*100:0)+'%';
  let cur=0;secs.forEach((s,i)=>{if(s.getBoundingClientRect().top<140)cur=i});
  links.forEach((a,i)=>a.classList.toggle('on',i===cur));
}
addEventListener('scroll',onScroll,{passive:true});onScroll();

// Exercise tabs
document.querySelectorAll('.tabs button').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{
  document.querySelectorAll('.tabs button').forEach(x=>x.classList.toggle('on',x===b));
  ['a','b'].forEach(k=>document.getElementById('t-'+k).hidden=k!==b.dataset.t);
});

// Reveal answers
document.querySelectorAll('.reveal').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{
  const a=b.nextElementSibling,s=a.classList.toggle('show');b.textContent=s?'Hide answer':'Show answer';
});

// Table + graph
const f=x=>x*x-6*x+7,xs=[0,1,2,3,4,5,6];
const fmt=n=>String(n).replace('-','−');
document.getElementById('tbl').innerHTML=
  '<tr><th>x</th>'+xs.map(x=>`<td class="pt" data-x="${x}">${x}</td>`).join('')+'</tr>'+
  '<tr><th>y</th>'+xs.map(x=>`<td class="pt" data-x="${x}">${fmt(f(x))}</td>`).join('')+'</tr>';

const svg=document.getElementById('graph'),W=640,H=420,x0=-1,x1=7,y0=-3,y1=8;
const X=x=>(x-x0)/(x1-x0)*W,Y=y=>H-(y-y0)/(y1-y0)*H,NS='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
let g='';
for(let x=x0;x<=x1;x++)g+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="0" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${H}" stroke="var(--line)"/>`;
for(let y=y0;y<=y1;y++)g+=`<line x1="0" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)"/>`;
g+=`<line x1="0" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${W}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="0" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${H}" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="2"/>`;
g+=`<line x1="${X(3)}" y1="0" x2="${X(3)}" y2="${H}" stroke="var(--gold)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/><text x="${X(3)+6}" y="18" fill="var(--gold)" font-size="14">x = 3</text>`;
for(let x=1;x<=6;x++)g+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${Y(0)+16}" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">${x}</text>`;
for(let y=-2;y<=7;y++)if(y)g+=`<text x="${X(0)-6}" y="${Y(y)+4}" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="12" text-anchor="end">${fmt(y)}</text>`;
let d='';for(let x=0;x<=6.001;x+=.05)d+=(d?'L':'M')+X(x).toFixed(1)+' '+Y(f(x)).toFixed(1);
g+=`<path d="${d}" fill="none" stroke="var(--blue)" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>`;
xs.forEach(x=>g+=`<circle class="dot" data-x="${x}" cx="${X(x)}" cy="${Y(f(x))}" r="${x===3?8:6}" fill="${x===3?'var(--red)':'var(--green)'}"/>`);
g+=`<text id="lbl" x="${X(3)+14}" y="${Y(-2)+5}" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700">Vertex (3, −2)</text>`;
svg.innerHTML=g;
function pick(x){
  document.querySelectorAll('td.pt').forEach(t=>t.classList.toggle('on',+t.dataset.x===x));
  document.querySelectorAll('.dot').forEach(c=>c.setAttribute('r',+c.dataset.x===x?10:(+c.dataset.x===3?8:6)));
  const l=document.getElementById('lbl'),px=X(x),py=Y(f(x));
  l.setAttribute('x',x>4?px-14:px+14);l.setAttribute('text-anchor',x>4?'end':'start');l.setAttribute('y',py+5);
  l.textContent=(x===3?'Vertex ':'Point ')+`(${x}, ${fmt(f(x))})`;
}
document.querySelectorAll('td.pt').forEach(t=>t.onclick=()=>pick(+t.dataset.x));
document.querySelectorAll('.dot').forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>pick(+c.dataset.x));
pick(3);
</script>
</body>
